<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter04-potentialflows" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Potential flows</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>The most basic introduction to fluid dynamics usually involves the study of two-dimensional incompressible and irrotational flows, otherwise known as <em>potential flows</em>. There is a good reason for this: the theory is elegant, visualisable, and uses to its benefit the enormous power of complex-variable theory. </p>

    <p>
      Previously, we defined the concept of vorticity via <m>\bomega = \nabla \times \bu</m>, which serves as a measure of the local angular velocity of the flow. It turns out that in 2D, irrotational flows, with <m>\omega = 0</m>, provide a powerful restriction to the complexity of flows, allowing the development of the above <em>potential flow theory</em>.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <caption>From "Fundamental Principles of Flows" and "Characteristics of the Laminar and Turbulent Flows" by Hunter Rouse. Courtesy of IIHR, University of Iowa. </caption> 
      <video youtube="glfm3NMMxh0"></video>
    </figure>

  </introduction>

<section xml:id="sec-Potential-and-streamfunction">
  <title>The velocity potential</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we focus on 2D flows where the velocity vector is given by 
    <me>
      \bu(x, y, t) = u(x, y, t)\bi + v(x, y, t)\bj = [u, v].
    </me>
    With the velocity given as above, the vorticity is then 
    <men xml:id="eqn-2d-vorticity">
      \bomega = \nabla \times \bu = \left(\pd{v}{x} - \pd{u}{y}\right)\bk.
    </men>
    If we assume that the flow is irrotational according to <xref ref="def-irrotational"/>, then <m>\nabla \times \bu = 0</m> and 
    <men xml:id="eqn-2d-irrotational">
      \pd{v}{x} - \pd{u}{y} = 0.
    </men>
  </p>

  <p>
    Further, we know that if the flow is irrotational, then there exists a <em>velocity potential</em>, <m>\phi</m>, such that <m>\bu = \nabla \phi</m>. Thus the velocities are expressed as 
    <men xml:id="eqn-vel-potential">
      u = \pd{\phi}{x} \quad \textrm{and} \quad v = \pd{\phi}{y}.
    </men>
  </p>

  <p>
    The above result about irrotational flows is a standard result in Vector Calculus, but we will re-state the result here for reference, and provide a review of its proof. 
  </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-exist-potential">
      <title>Existence of a potential</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider a three-dimensional time-dependent velocity field, <m>\bu = \bu(\bx, t)</m> defined on a simply connected domain <m>\bx\in D \subset \mathbb{R}^3</m>.
        </p>
          
          <p>Then <m>\bu</m> is irrotational, i.e. <m>\nabla \times \bu = \mathbf{0}</m>, if and only if there exists a scalar potential <m>\phi</m>, defined on <m>D \times \mathbb{R}^+</m>, such that <m>\bu = \nabla \phi</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <proof>
        <title>Proof (non-examinable)</title>
        <p>
          Define 
          <me>
            \phi(\bx, t) \equiv \phi_0(t) + \int_C \bu \cdot \de\bx,
          </me>
          where <m>C</m> is any contour connecting an arbitrary origin point to the point <m>\bx</m> (changing the origin point will change the "constant" of integration <m>\phi_0(t)</m>).
        </p>

        <p>
          We can verify, using the definition of differentiation, and the fundamental theorem of calculus, applied along each of the three coordinate directions, that <m>\nabla \phi = \bu</m> as desired.
        </p>

        <p>
          The key is to prove that the above definition is independent of the choice of contour <m>C</m>. To this end, consider two contours, <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m>, both with the same origin point, <m>O</m>, and end point <m>P</m>. Then the contour <m>C_1 - C_2</m> is a closed contour beginning and ending at <m>O</m>. 
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-potential-proof">
          <caption>Proof of the uniqueness of the potential</caption>
          <image source="potentialproof.jpg" width="80%"/>
        </figure>

        <p>
          By Stokes' theorem, 
          <me>
            \oint_{C_1 - C_2} \bu \cdot \de\bx = \iint_S (\nabla \times \bu) \cdot \bn \, \de{S}, 
          </me>
          where <m>S</m> is any surface with bounding curve <m>C_1 - C_2</m> (such a surface lying in <m>D</m> exists because <m>D</m> is simply connected), and with unit normal <m>\bn</m> positively oriented with the bounding curve. However, the right-hand side is zero by irrotationality, and therefore 
          <me>
            \int_{C_1} \bu \cdot \de\bx = \int_{C_2} \bu \cdot \de\bx,
          </me>
          and our choice of curve in the definition of the potential is irrelevant. 
        </p>

        <p>
          The converse direction of the theorem follows directly from the fact that "curl grad equals zero", i.e. <m>\nabla \times \nabla \phi = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

  <p>
    Let us return to discussing the setting of potential flow.
  </p>

  <p>
    In addition to being irrotational, we further assume that the flow is incompressible. Therefore from <xref ref="def-incompressible"/>,
    <men xml:id="eqn-2d-divu">
      \nabla \cdot \bu = \pd{u}{x} + \pd{v}{y} = \pd{^2\phi}{x^2} + \pd{^2\phi}{y^2} = 0.
    </men>
    This is the crucial result, which is that in potential flows, we need only solve the Laplace equation: 
    <men xml:id="eqn-2d-laplace">
      \nabla^2 \phi = 0,
    </men>
    within the flow region. This is effectively a single linear equation for the single unknown <m>\phi</m>. However, for different problems, the boundary conditions can render even this "simple" problem difficult. 
  </p>

  <p>
  Once the velocity potential <m>\phi</m> has been found, the velocities in the flow can be recovered from the relationship <xref ref="eqn-vel-potential"/>. The pressure in the flow also follows from Bernoulli's equation. For the situation of a steady potential flow, following <xref ref="thm-bernoulli-potential"/>, it is 
  <men xml:id="eqn-2d-bernoulli">
    \frac{p}{\rho} + \frac{1}{2} |\nabla \phi|^2 + \chi = \textrm{constant}.
  </men>
  </p>
</introduction>

  <subsection>
    <title>Elementary flows</title>

    <p>The next three examples will introduce you to the elementary flows consisting of uniform flow, stagnation point flow, and line source/sink flows. You will also investigate the notion of a source "strength".</p>
    
    <example xml:id="example-potential-uniform">
      <title>Uniform flow</title>
      <idx><h>uniform flow</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the velocity potential <m>\phi</m> and the velocity <m>\bu</m> of a uniform flow of speed <m>U</m> directed at angle <m>\alpha</m> to the <m>x</m>-axis.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The velocity is constant, <m>\bu = U[\cos\alpha, \sin\alpha]</m>. Integrating <m>\pd{\phi}{x} = U\cos\alpha</m> and <m>\pd{\phi}{y} = U\sin\alpha</m> gives, up to a constant, the linear function
          <me>
            \phi(x, y) = Ux \cos\alpha + Uy \sin\alpha,
          </me>
          which clearly satisfies <m>\nabla^2\phi = 0</m>. The streamlines are shown below.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-potential-uniform">
          <caption>Streamlines (or velocity field) of uniform flow with <m>U = 1</m> and <m>\alpha = \pi/4</m>.</caption>
          <image source="uniformflow" width="90%"/> 
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>
  
    <example xml:id="example-potential-stagnation">
      <title>Stagnation point flow</title><idx><h>stagnation point flow</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>\phi = \frac{1}{2} (x^2 - y^2)</m> is the velocity potential of an incompressible flow, and find the velocity <m>\bu</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Since <m>\pd{^2\phi}{x^2} + \pd{^2\phi}{y^2} = 1 - 1 = 0</m>, Laplace's equation is satisfied. The velocity is
          <me>
            \bu = \nabla\phi = [x, -y],
          </me>
          which vanishes at the origin. This is <em>stagnation point flow</em>, shown below.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-potential-stagnation">
          <caption>Streamlines (or velocity field) of stagnation point flow.</caption>
          <image source="stagnationflow" width="90%"/> 
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>
  
    <example xml:id="example-potential-linesource">
      <title>Line source</title>
      <idx><h>line source</h></idx> 
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>line source</em> ejects fluid from the origin equally in all directions. Find a radially symmetric potential <m>\phi = \phi(r)</m> satisfying Laplace's equation, and the corresponding velocity <m>\bu</m>. By computing the flux of fluid across a circle centred at the origin, relate the constant in <m>\phi</m> to the rate <m>Q</m> at which fluid is produced.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          In plane polar coordinates,
          <me>
            \nabla^2 \phi = \frac{1}{r} \pd{}{r}\left(r \pd{\phi}{r}\right) + \frac{1}{r^2} \pd{^2 \phi}{\theta^2} = 0.
          </me>
          With <m>\phi = \phi(r)</m>, direct integration gives
          <me>
            \phi = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log r,
          </me>
          where we have set an additive constant to zero, and written the leading constant as <m>Q/(2\pi)</m> in anticipation of the result below. The velocity is
          <me>
            \bu = \nabla \phi = \pd{\phi}{r} \be_r + \frac{1}{r}\pd{\phi}{\theta} \be_{\theta} = \frac{Q}{2\pi r} \be_r = \frac{Q}{2\pi r^2} [x, y],
          </me>
          using <m>\be_r = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m>. The flow is directed radially outwards. It is called a "line" source because in <m>(x, y, z)</m> the source runs parallel to the <m>z</m>-axis.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-potential-linesource">
          <caption>Streamlines (or velocity field) of line source flow.</caption>
          <image source="linesource" width="90%"/> 
        </figure>

        <p>
          The flux (the flow per unit time) of fluid crossing a closed contour <m>C</m> with unit normal <m>\bn</m> is <m>\int_C \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{s}</m>. Take <m>C</m> to be the circle <m>r = a</m>, on which <m>\bn = \be_r</m> and the arc-length element is <m>\de{s} = a \, \de\theta</m>. Then
          <me>
            \int_C \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{s} = \int_0^{2\pi} \frac{Q}{2\pi a} \, (a \, \de\theta) = Q.
          </me>
          Therefore, <m>Q</m>, the <em>strength</em> of the source, is the rate at which fluid is produced. If <m>Q \lt 0</m>, we refer to the flow as a <em>line sink</em>.
          <idx><h>line sink</h></idx>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      Crucially, the governing equation is Laplace's equation, which is linear, so any sum of elementary flows is also an admissible flow.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="example-potential-movingsource">
      <title>Line source in a uniform flow</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the velocity potential <m>\phi</m> and the velocity <m>\bu</m> of a line source of strength <m>Q</m> at the origin placed in a uniform flow of speed <m>U</m> in the <m>x</m>-direction.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Adding the potentials of <xref ref="example-potential-uniform"/> (with <m>\alpha = 0</m>) and <xref ref="example-potential-linesource"/>,
          <me>
            \phi = Ux + \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log r = Ux + \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log \sqrt{x^2 + y^2},
          </me>
          and so
          <me>
            \bu = [U, 0] + \frac{Q}{2\pi(x^2 + y^2)} [x, y].
          </me>
          The streamlines are shown below. Where do you think the stagnation point lies in this flow?
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-potential-movinglinesource">
          <caption>Streamlines (or velocity field) of a line source in a uniform flow with <m>U = 1</m> and <m>Q = 1</m>.</caption>
          <image source="movinglinesource" width="90%"/> 
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

  </subsection>
</section>

  <section xml:id="sec-streamfunction">
   <title>The streamfunction</title>
<introduction>
   <p>
    Our next task is to introduce the concept of the <em>streamfunction</em>. Remember that in 2D, the irrotational flow led to the equation <xref ref="eqn-2d-irrotational"/> and this led to the existence of the potential function. If we begin with incompressibility, however, we have <xref ref="eqn-2d-divu"/>, which can be written as 
    <men xml:id="eqn-1d-divu-cross">
      \big(\nabla \times [-v, u, 0]\big)\cdot\bk = \pd{u}{x} + \pd{v}{y} = 0.
    </men>
    We can therefore deduce the existence of an analogous function, the <em>streamfunction</em>, <m>\psi(x, y, t)</m>, satisfying 
    <men xml:id="eqn-2d-uv-streamfunction">
      u = \pd{\psi}{y} \quad \textrm{and} \quad v = -\pd{\psi}{x}.
    </men>
  Alternatively and more conveniently, we can write 
    <men xml:id="eqn-2d-streamfunction">
      \bu = \nabla \times (\psi \bk).
    </men>
    To establish the existence of the streamfunction, we follow a similar proof as in <xref ref="thm-exist-potential"/> but now with the definition that 
   <men xml:id="eqn-streamfunction-def-psi">
    \psi(x, y, t) = \psi_0(t) + \int_0^{\bx} (u \de{y} - v \de{x}),
   </men>
   where again <m>\psi_0(t)</m> is an arbitrary function of <m>t</m>. The proof is otherwise identical, relying on establishing the independence of path of the integral using Stokes' theorem.  
   </p>

   <p>
    Why all this work? The streamfunction has an intuitive interpretation via the following result. </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-streamfunction">
      <title>The streamfunction is constant along streamlines</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The streamfunction, <m>\psi(x, y, t)</m>, is constant along streamlines of the flow (i.e. curves that are everywhere tangent to <m>\bu</m> at a fixed time <m>t</m>).
        </p>
      </statement>

      <proof>
        <p>
          The proof follows simply by the fact that
          <me>
            \bu \cdot \nabla \psi = \left(\pd{\psi}{y}, -\pd{\psi}{x}\right) \cdot \left(\pd{\psi}{x}, \pd{\psi}{y}\right) = 0,
          </me>
          where the first equality uses <xref ref="eqn-2d-uv-streamfunction"/>. 
        </p>

        <p>
          Since <m>\nabla\psi</m> is normal to the level sets of <m>\psi</m>, and <m>\bu</m> is orthogonal to <m>\nabla\psi</m>, the velocity <m>\bu</m> is tangent to the level sets. These level sets are therefore streamlines.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>


   <p>
    A graphical depiction of the property in <xref ref="thm-streamfunction"/> is shown below.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-streamfunction">
      <caption>Flow between two streamlines, the streamlines <m>\psi = \psi_1</m> and <m>\psi = \psi_2</m>. Later, we will consider the flux through the contour <m>C</m> illustrated in the figure along with its unit normal <m>\bn</m>.</caption>
      <image source="streamfunction.jpg" width="70%"/>
    </figure>


      <!-- <figure>
        <image source="uniformflow" width="90%"/> 
          <caption>Streamlines (or velocity field) of uniform flow with <m>U = 1</m> and <m>\alpha = \pi/4</m>.</caption>
        </figure> -->

   

   <p>
    The streamfunction is thus constant on streamlines. Consider two streamlines. The following theorem relates the flux between the streamlines to the values of <m>\psi</m> on them. 
   </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-flux-streamlines">
      <title>Flux between streamlines</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
        Consider two streamlines <m>\psi = \psi_1</m> and <m>\psi = \psi_2</m>. We assume that the streamlines pass through the points A and B respectively. Consider a contour <m>C</m> connecting A and B. The <em>flux</em> (net flow of fluid) through <m>C</m> and hence between the streamlines is
        <me>
          |\textrm{flux}| = |\psi_2 - \psi_1|.
        </me>
        We have not specified the sign of the flux as it depends on the direction chosen through <m>C</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

        <proof>
          <p>
            By definition, the flux is given by the integral 
            <me>
              \int_C \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{s},
            </me>
            where <m>C</m> is any smooth path joining the two streamlines with unit normal <m>\bn</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-streamfunction"/>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note that given the curve, and in consideration of a small arclength element <m>\de{s}</m>, the tangent and normal are given by 
            <me>
              \bt = \frac{[\de{x}, \de{y}]}{\sqrt{\de{x}^2 + \de{y}^2}} = \frac{[\de{x}, \de{y}]}{\de{s}}, \qquad 
              \bn = \frac{[\de{y}, -\de{x}]}{\de{s}}.
            </me>
            Therefore we can write <m>\bn \de{s} = [\de{y}, -\de{x}]</m>. Then the flux is re-written as the following: 
            <md>
              <mrow>\textrm{flux} \amp= \int_C \left[\pd{\psi}{y}, -\pd{\psi}{x}\right] \cdot [\de{y}, -\de{x}], </mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \int_C \left(\pd{\psi}{x} \, \de{x} + \pd{\psi}{y} \, \de{y} \right), </mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \bigl[ \psi \bigr]_C, </mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \psi(B) - \psi(A), </mrow>
              <mrow>\amp= \psi_2 - \psi_1. </mrow>
            </md>
          In the third line above, <m>[\psi]_C</m> is the change of <m>\psi</m> along the contour.  
          Note that there is a somewhat arbitrary choice of direction for the contour <m>C</m>, as related to the selection of the normal direction <m>\bn</m>, and the positivity or negativity of the flux. To be safe, we have taken the absolute value in the statement of the theorem.
        </p>
        </proof>

    </theorem>

    <p>
      An example of the use of the above theorem is given in <xref ref="ex-hw-potential-fluxcalc"/>. 
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Flux from the streamfunction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let the velocity of a two-dimensional flow be given by the formula
          <me>
            \bu = [3ax^2 - 3ay^2, \, -6axy]
          </me>
          where <m>a</m> is a positive constant. By finding the streamfunction, <m>\psi</m>, calculate the volume flux across a curve connecting points <m>A = (0, 0)</m> and <m>B = (1, 1)</m> using <xref ref="thm-flux-streamlines"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We can check, using the integration of velocity components that 
          <me>
            \psi = 3ax^2 y - a y^3 + C.
          </me>
          Hence the flux is
          <me>
            Q = \psi(B) - \psi(A) = 2a.
          </me>
          We can verify this flux should be positive if the normal is oriented to the right of the curve from A to B. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
      
    
    <p>
        Finally, note that the velocity potential was governed by Laplace's equation, <m>\nabla^2 \phi = 0</m>. The streamfunction is also governed by the same Laplace's equation. 
    </p>
       
   <theorem xml:id="thm-streamfunction-laplace">
          <title>Streamfunction satisfies Laplace's equation</title>
          <statement>
            <p>
              If the flow is also irrotational, then, like the velocity potential, the streamfunction satisfies Laplace's equation: 
              <men xml:id="eqn-2d-streamfunction-laplace">
                \pd{^2 \psi}{x^2} + \pd{^2 \psi}{y^2} = \nabla^2 \psi = 0.
              </men>
            </p>
          </statement>

            <proof>
              <p>
                Substitute the relationship <xref ref="eqn-2d-uv-streamfunction"/> into <xref ref="eqn-2d-irrotational"/>.
              </p>
            </proof>
        </theorem>
   
   <p>
    For some flows, such as the line source in <xref ref="example-potential-linesource"/>, it is easier to work in polar coordinates. Since we know that <m>\bu = \nabla \times (\psi \bk)</m> by <xref ref="eqn-2d-streamfunction"/>, we can use the conversion of the curl to polar coordinates in <xref ref="eqn-identity-curlpolar"/> to give 
    <men xml:id="eqn-streamfunction-polar">
      u_r = \frac{1}{r} \pd{\psi}{\theta} \quad \textrm{and} \quad u_{\theta} = -\pd{\psi}{r},
    </men>
   which allows us to relate the radial and angular velocities to the streamfunction. 
   </p>
</introduction>

   <subsection>
    <title>Elementary flows</title>

   <p>
    Let us return to each of the examples in <xref ref="sec-Potential-and-streamfunction"/> and reconsider their corresponding streamfunctions. 
   </p>

    <example>
      <title>Uniform flow</title>
      <idx><h>uniform flow, streamfunction</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the streamfunction <m>\psi</m> for the uniform flow of <xref ref="example-potential-uniform"/>, and sketch its streamlines.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Integrating <m>u = \psi_y = U\cos\alpha</m> and <m>v = -\psi_x = U\sin\alpha</m> gives, up to an arbitrary constant,
          <me>
            \psi = Uy\cos\alpha - Ux\sin\alpha.
          </me>
          Lines of constant <m>\psi</m> are <m>y = x\tan\alpha + \textrm{constant}</m>, as in <xref ref="fig-potential-uniform"/>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <title>Stagnation point flow</title><idx><h>stagnation point flow, streamfunction</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the streamfunction <m>\psi</m> for the stagnation point flow of <xref ref="example-potential-stagnation"/>, and sketch its streamlines.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Integrating <m>u = \psi_y = x</m> and <m>v = -\psi_x = -y</m> gives, up to an arbitrary constant, <m>\psi = xy</m>. Curves of constant <m>\psi</m> are the hyperbolae shown in <xref ref="fig-potential-stagnation"/>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

  <example xml:id="example-streamfunction-linesource">
    <title>Line source</title>
    <idx><h>line source, streamfunction</h></idx> 
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the streamfunction <m>\psi</m> for the line source of <xref ref="example-potential-linesource"/>, and sketch its streamlines.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        From <xref ref="eqn-identity-grad-polar"/>, the velocity is entirely radial,
        <men xml:id="eqn-streamfunction-u-linesource">
         \bu = \pd{\phi}{r}\be_r = \frac{Q}{2\pi r} \be_r.
        </men>
        Using <xref ref="eqn-streamfunction-polar"/>, we integrate <m>\frac{1}{r} \pd{\psi}{\theta} = \frac{Q}{2\pi r}</m> (with <m>\pd{\psi}{r} = -u_\theta = 0</m>) to give
        <me>
          \psi = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \theta.
        </me>
        The lines of constant <m>\psi</m> are the radial lines of constant <m>\theta</m>, as in <xref ref="fig-potential-linesource"/>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
   
<p>
    Note that <m>\psi</m> is a multi-valued function in <xref ref="example-streamfunction-linesource"/>, gaining a jump of <m>Q</m> every time the origin is encircled. This is a warning that the standard proof, analogous to <xref ref="thm-exist-potential"/>, leading to the existence of a unique streamfunction via <xref ref="eqn-streamfunction-def-psi"/>, would not apply, because the flow domain (the plane with the origin removed) is not simply connected. However, despite this, the streamfunction provides well-defined predictions of streamlines on the <em>cut</em> plane with e.g. <m>\theta \in [0, 2\pi)</m>.
   </p>

  <example>
    <title>Line source in a uniform flow</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the streamfunction <m>\psi</m> for the line source in a uniform flow of <xref ref="example-potential-movingsource"/>, and the equation of its streamlines.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        By linearity, we add the streamfunctions of the uniform flow (with <m>\alpha = 0</m>) and the line source:
        <me>
          \psi = Uy + \frac{Q}{2\pi}\theta.
        </me>
        The streamlines are given by
        <me>
          Uy + \frac{Q}{2\pi} \theta = \frac{Q}{2\pi}C,
        </me>
        having chosen the constant on the right-hand side for convenience. Then using <m>y = r\sin\theta</m>, we have
        <me>
          r = \left(\frac{Q}{2\pi U}\right) \frac{C - \theta}{\sin\theta}.
        </me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

<p>
  Our previous examples were reliant on considering the flows generated by the velocity potentials studied previously. However, we can also consider "fundamental solutions" of the equation <m>\nabla^2 \psi = 0</m> in their own right. Recall that in deriving the velocity potential of the line source in <xref ref="example-potential-linesource"/>, we considered a radially symmetric problem, where <m>\phi = \phi(r)</m> is only dependent on the distance from the origin. The same argument shows that a radially symmetric streamfunction <m>\psi = \psi(r)</m> is also a solution. This leads us to the following example. 
</p>

  <example xml:id="example-streamfunction-linevortex">
   <title>Line vortex </title>
   <idx><h>line vortex</h></idx>
   <statement>
     <p>
       The radially symmetric streamfunction
       <men xml:id="eqn-streamfunction-linevortex">
         \psi = -\frac{\Gamma}{2\pi}\log r,
       </men>
       defined up to a constant, corresponds to a <em>line vortex</em>. Find its velocity <m>\bu</m>, and compute the circulation around a circle of radius <m>a</m> centred at the origin.
     </p>
   </statement>

   <solution>
     <p>
       The streamlines are circles of constant <m>r</m>, shown in <xref ref="fig-streamfunction-linevortex"/>. From <xref ref="eqn-streamfunction-polar"/>,
       <men xml:id="eq-u-line-vortex">
         \bu = -\pd{\psi}{r} \be_{\theta} = \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi r} \be_{\theta},
       </men>
       so the fluid orbits the origin, with speed <m>|\Gamma|/(2\pi r)</m> increasing as <m>r \to 0</m>.
     </p>

     <figure xml:id="fig-streamfunction-linevortex">
       <caption>Streamlines (or velocity field) of a line vortex with <m>\Gamma = 1</m>.</caption>
       <image source="linevortex" width="90%"/> 
     </figure>

     <p>
       The circulation around a contour <m>C</m> is
       <me>
         \oint_C \bu \cdot \de{\bx} = \oint_C (u \, \de{x} + v \, \de{y}),
       </me>
       i.e. the sum of the velocity components tangential to <m>C</m>. By Stokes' theorem it equals the flux of vorticity through any surface bounded by <m>C</m>. Since the flow is irrotational away from the origin, all of this vorticity is concentrated at the origin. On the circle <m>r = a</m>, <m>\de{\bx} = a \be_{\theta} \de{\theta}</m>, so
       <men xml:id="eq-line-vortex-circulation">
         \oint_C \bu \cdot \de{\bx} = \int_0^{2\pi} \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi a} \be_{\theta} \cdot \be_{\theta} a \de\theta = \Gamma.
       </men>
       The quantity <m>\Gamma</m> is called the <em>vortex strength</em>, analogous to the source strength <m>Q</m> in <xref ref="example-potential-linesource"/>. Notice that <m>\Gamma \gt 0</m> corresponds to flow in the anticlockwise sense, and <m>\Gamma \lt 0</m> to flow in the clockwise sense.
     </p>
   </solution>
  </example>

   <p>
    In <xref ref="ex-hw-streamfunction-flux-compute"/>, you will practise the computation of fluxes using line integrals. 
   </p>
</subsection>
</section>

<section xml:id="sec-complex-potential">
  <title>The complex potential</title>
<introduction>
  <aside>
    <title>Changed in 2026</title>
    <p>This section was streamlined and shortened.</p>
  </aside>

 <p>
  In the previous two sections, we studied the velocity potential <m>\phi</m> and streamfunction <m>\psi</m> of two-dimensional potential flows using real-valued Vector Calculus. You may have noticed a certain symmetry between the formulae for <m>\phi</m> and <m>\psi</m>. Complex analysis makes this symmetry transparent, and provides a much more elegant and powerful framework for studying two-dimensional flow.
 </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Examinable content</title>
    <p>
      The proofs in this section are non-examinable, but you are expected to understand the theorems and their relevance to potential flow.
    </p>
  </remark>
</introduction>

 <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-introduction">
  <title>Analytic functions and Laplace's equation</title>

 <p>
  We write a complex function as <m>f(z) = u(x, y) + \im v(x, y)</m>, where <m>z = x + \im y</m> and <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are the real and imaginary parts of <m>f</m>, and use subscripts for partial derivatives, e.g. <m>u_x = \pd{u}{x}</m>. The derivative is defined as for real functions,
  <men xml:id="eqn-complex-derivative">
    f'(z) = \lim_{\Delta z \to 0} \frac{f(z + \Delta z) - f(z)}{\Delta z},
  </men>
  with the crucial difference that the limit must exist, and take the same value, however <m>\Delta z \to 0</m> in the complex plane. For further details, see <xref ref="ref-kreyszig"/> and <xref ref="ref-fornberg"/>.
 </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-analytic">
      <title>Analyticity</title>
      <idx><h>analytic</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A function <m>f</m> is <em>analytic</em> in a domain if it is differentiable at every point of the domain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-CR1">
      <title>Cauchy-Riemann equations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
         If <m>f(z) = u(x, y) + \im v(x, y)</m> is analytic in a domain, then the Cauchy-Riemann equations
       <men xml:id="eqn-CR">
       u_x = v_y \quad \text{and} \quad u_y = -v_x
       </men>
       hold in that domain. Conversely, if <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> have continuous first partial derivatives satisfying <xref ref="eqn-CR"/>, then <m>f</m> is analytic.
        </p>
      </statement>

        <proof>
          <title>Proof (non-examinable)</title>
          <p> 
            Approaching <m>z</m> along the real direction, <m>\Delta z = \Delta x</m>, the definition <xref ref="eqn-complex-derivative"/> gives <m>f'(z) = u_x + \im v_x</m>. Approaching along the imaginary direction, <m>\Delta z = \im \Delta y</m>, gives <m>f'(z) = -\im u_y + v_y</m>. Equating the real and imaginary parts of the two results yields the Cauchy-Riemann equations. For the converse, see <xref ref="ref-kreyszig"/>.
          </p>
        </proof>
    </theorem>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-complex-laplace">
    <title>Analyticity and Laplace's equation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      If <m>f(z) = u(x, y) + \im v(x, y)</m> is analytic in a domain <m>D</m>, then <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> satisfy Laplace's equation, 
      <me>
        \nabla^2 u = u_{xx} + u_{yy} = 0 \quad \textrm{and} \quad
        \nabla^2 v = v_{xx} + v_{yy} = 0,
      </me>
      in <m>D</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

      <proof>
        <title>Proof (non-examinable)</title>
        <p>
          Analytic functions are infinitely differentiable (see <xref ref="ref-kreyszig"/>), so we may differentiate <xref ref="eqn-CR"/> to obtain
          <me>
            u_{xx} = v_{yx} \quad \text{and} \quad -u_{yy} = v_{xy}. 
          </me>
          The second derivatives are continuous, so <m>v_{xy} = v_{yx}</m>, and therefore <m>u_{xx} + u_{yy} = 0</m>. Laplace's equation is analogously proved for <m>v</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
  </theorem>

<p>
  This remarkable result is a major reason why complex analysis played such an important role in applied mathematics and physics, since Laplace's equation governs gravitation, electrostatics, fluid mechanics, and more. In particular, any function that is analytic in a region, whether a polynomial, exponential, logarithm, or otherwise, can be associated with (some kind of) potential flow there.
</p>
 </subsection>

 <subsection xml:id="subsec-complex-examples">
  <title>Examples of elementary flows</title>

  <p>
    Let us return to the examples of flows studied in <xref ref="sec-Potential-and-streamfunction"/> and re-interpret them using the theory of analytic functions.
  </p>

  <p>
    We will associate the velocity potential, <m>\phi</m>, and streamfunction <m>\psi</m> with an analytic function in the following way. 
  </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-complex-potential">
      <title>Complex potential</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Let <m>\phi(x,y)</m> and <m>\psi(x, y)</m> be the respective velocity potential and streamfunction for some potential flow. We define
    <men xml:id="eqn-complex-potential">
      f(z) = \phi(x, y) + \im \psi(x, y),
    </men>
    and call <m>f</m> the <em>complex potential</em>. Since <m>\phi_x = \psi_y = u</m> and <m>\phi_y = -\psi_x = v</m>, the functions <m>\phi</m> and <m>\psi</m> satisfy the Cauchy-Riemann equations, so <m>f</m> is analytic.
        </p>

        <p>
          Note that by differentiating in the horizontal and vertical directions, we have 
          <me>
            \dd{f}{z} = \pd{}{x} (\phi + \im \psi) = -\im \pd{}{y} (\phi + \im \psi). 
          </me>
         Therefore it follows that the horizontal and vertical velocity components of the flow, related via <m>\bu = [u, v]</m>, are given by 
         <men xml:id="eqn-complex-velocity">
          \dd{f}{z} = u - \im v.
         </men>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
      <title>Uniform flow</title>
      <idx><h>uniform flow, complex</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the complex potential of the uniform flow of <xref ref="example-potential-uniform"/> is <m>f(z) = U \e^{-\im \alpha} z</m>, and recover <m>\phi</m>, <m>\psi</m> and <m>\bu</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          With <m>z = x + \im y</m>,
          <me>
            U\e^{-\im\alpha}z = U(x\cos\alpha + y\sin\alpha) + \im U(y\cos\alpha - x\sin\alpha),
          </me>
          whose real and imaginary parts are the <m>\phi</m> and <m>\psi</m> of <xref ref="example-potential-uniform"/> and the corresponding example in <xref ref="sec-streamfunction"/>. Also <m>f'(z) = U\e^{-\im\alpha} = U\cos\alpha - \im U\sin\alpha</m>, so <m>\bu = U[\cos\alpha, \sin\alpha]</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  
    <example>
      <title>Stagnation point flow</title>
      <idx><h>stagnation point flow, complex</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the complex potential of the stagnation point flow of <xref ref="example-potential-stagnation"/> is <m>f(z) = z^2/2</m>, and recover <m>\phi</m>, <m>\psi</m> and <m>\bu</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have <m>z^2/2 = \frac12(x^2 - y^2) + \im xy</m>, so <m>\phi = \frac12(x^2 - y^2)</m> and <m>\psi = xy</m>. Also <m>f'(z) = z = x + \im y = u - \im v</m>, so <m>\bu = [x, -y]</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  
    <example>
      <title>Line source</title>
      <idx><h>line source, complex</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the complex potential of the line source of <xref ref="example-potential-linesource"/> is
          <men xml:id="eqn-complex-linesource">
            f(z) = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log z,
          </men>
          and recover <m>\phi</m>, <m>\psi</m> and <m>\bu</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The complex logarithm is not analytic at <m>z = 0</m>, a <em>branch point</em>, but is analytic away from the origin once a branch cut is chosen. Writing <m>z = r\e^{\im \theta}</m> in polar form, it is evaluated via
          <men xml:id="eqn-complex-logarithm">
            \log z = \log r + \im \theta.
          </men>
          Hence
          <me>
            \phi = \Re[f] = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log r \quad \text{and} \quad
            \psi = \Im[f] = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \theta.
          </me>
          Indeed, the streamlines are the rays <m>\theta = \textrm{constant}</m>. From <xref ref="eqn-complex-velocity"/>, multiplying the top and bottom by the conjugate of <m>z</m>,
          <me>
           u - \im v = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \frac{1}{z} = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \frac{x - \im y}{x^2 + y^2}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  
    <example xml:id="example-complexpotential-linevortex">
      <title>Line vortex flow</title>
      <idx><h>line vortex, complex</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the complex potential of the line vortex of <xref ref="example-streamfunction-linevortex"/> is
          <men xml:id="eq-line-vortex-complex-potential">
            f(z) = -\frac{\im \Gamma}{2\pi} \log z,
          </men>
          and recover <m>\phi</m>, <m>\psi</m> and <m>\bu</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Using <xref ref="eqn-complex-logarithm"/> with <m>z = r\e^{\im\theta}</m>,
          <me>
            f(z) = \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi} (\theta - \im \log r),
          </me>
          so <m>\phi = \Gamma\theta/(2\pi)</m> and <m>\psi = -(\Gamma/2\pi) \log r</m>, which is constant on circles <m>r = \textrm{constant}</m>. The velocity follows from
          <me>
            u - \im v = -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi z} = -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi r}\e^{-\im\theta},
          </me>
          so <m>u + \im v = \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi r}\im\e^{\im\theta}</m>, i.e. <m>\bu = \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi r}\be_\theta</m>, as in <xref ref="eq-u-line-vortex"/>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  
 </subsection>
</section>

<section xml:id="sec-method-of-images">
  <title>The method of images</title>

  <introduction>
  <p>
    The preceding sections may give the misleading impression that solving potential-flow problems for two-dimensional flows is easy. This is not the case, and the primary reason is the presence of <em>boundary conditions</em>. The elementary flows we have previously considered were unconfined and/or we did not consider additional constraints on their behaviours at infinity. In reality, a real physical fluid, whether in the ocean, the air, or in a container, is confined in some direction, and we must often consider subtle questions about the mechanism that produces the fluid motion. 
  </p>

  <p>
    In this section, we consider the situation of solving for the potential flow in a fluid region with boundaries. Recall that this is equivalent to finding a velocity potential satisfying <m>\nabla^2 \phi = 0</m> or an analytic complex potential, <m>f(z)</m>. 
  </p>

  <p>
    Recall from <xref ref="note-noflux-BC"/> that on solid boundaries, we must impose the no-flux condition that
    <me>
      \bu \cdot \bn = \nabla \phi \cdot \bn = 0 \quad \text{on $\partial V$}.
    </me>
  </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-images-planar">
    <title>Planar boundaries: a half-plane</title>

   <p>
    Consider the situation illustrated in <xref ref="image-planar01"/>.
   </p> 
    <figure xml:id="image-planar01">
      <caption>A flow region in the right half-plane, with a single source placed at <m>x = d</m>.</caption>
      <image source="image-planar01.jpg" width="80%"/>
    </figure>
   
   <p>
    We envision a semi-infinite region of fluid bounded on the left by a wall at <m>x = 0</m>. A single (line) source of strength <m>Q</m> is placed at <m>x = d</m>. Therefore from <xref ref="eqn-complex-linesource"/>, we would expect that at least near <m>x = d</m>, the complex potential behaves as 
    <me>
      f(z) \sim \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log (z-d) \quad \text{as $z \to d$}.
    </me>
    However, the above potential does not satisfy the required boundary condition at <m>x = 0</m>, since its horizontal velocity penetrates through <m>x = 0</m>. This can be checked by inspecting the velocity or the streamlines (see <xref ref="ex-hw-image-planar01"/>).
   </p>

   <p>
    Rephrased in terms of the streamlines, the boundary condition at <m>x = 0</m> is equivalent to the constraint that 
    <me>
      \Im f(z) = \psi = \textrm{constant at $x = 0$}.
    </me>
   </p>

   <p>
    The solution to this problem uses <em>the method of images</em>.</p>

    <note xml:id="note-method-of-images">
      <title>Method of images</title>

      <p>
        Given a potential flow problem, we consider the superposition of elementary sinks/sources, i.e. 
       <me>
        f(z) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} Q_j \log(z - z_j), 
       </me>
      and/or vortices,
      <me>
        f(z) = -\frac{\im}{2\pi} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} \Gamma_j \log(z - z_j).
      </me>
      The strengths and locations of the individual contributions are chosen so that boundary conditions on the required boundaries (including at infinity) can be met. 
      </p>
    </note>
   
   <p>
    Notice that the <em>linearity</em> of the potential flow problem is crucial: any analytic function is associated with a velocity potential that satisfies Laplace's equation, <m>\nabla^2 \phi = 0</m>, and therefore the superposition of such functions also yields a permissible complex potential, <m>f</m>. 
   </p>

    <example xml:id="example-images-source-wall">
      <title>Line source near a wall</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the complex potential of a line source of strength <m>Q</m> at <m>z = d</m> in the half-plane <m>x \gt 0</m>, bounded by a wall at <m>x = 0</m>, and verify the no-flux condition on the wall.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We add a "fictitious" image source of the same strength at the reflected point <m>z = -d</m>, which lies outside the fluid region. This gives the complex potential
          <me>
            f(z) = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log(z - d) + \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log(z + d),
          </me>
          illustrated in <xref ref="fig-image-planar02"/>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-image-planar02">
          <caption>Placement of the image source at <m>x = -d</m> ensures that the boundary condition on <m>x = 0</m> is satisfied.</caption>
          <image source="image-planar02.jpg" width="80%"/>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The corresponding complex velocity is
          <me>
            u - \im v = f'(z) = \frac{Q}{\pi}\frac{z}{z^2 - d^2}.
          </me>
          On the wall <m>x = 0</m>, we have <m>z = \im y</m>, and
          <me>
            u - \im v = - \im \frac{Q}{\pi} \frac{y}{y^2 + d^2},
          </me>
          so the velocity is entirely vertical. Hence the condition <m>\bu \cdot \bn = 0</m> on the wall is satisfied.
        </p>

        <p>
          To develop an equation for the streamlines, we must first navigate the fact that the complex logarithm is only well-defined in a slit complex plane. Let
          <me>
            z - d = r_1 \e^{\im \theta_1} \quad \textrm{and} \quad z + d = r_2 \e^{\im \theta_2},
          </me>
          as shown in <xref ref="fig-image-duallog"/>. Using <xref ref="eqn-complex-logarithm"/>,
          <me>
            f(z) = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \left[ \log r_1 + \log r_2\right] + \im \frac{Q}{2\pi}\left[ \theta_1 + \theta_2 \right].
          </me>
          For each logarithm to be well defined, the angles must be restricted to an interval of length <m>2\pi</m>. We thus restrict <m>\theta_1 \in [0, 2\pi)</m> and <m>\theta_2 \in [-\pi, \pi)</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-image-duallog">
          <caption>When considering the evaluation of the flow, we must take care of the fact that the logarithm is multi-valued. A branch cut from each of the two branch points is imposed.</caption>
          <image source="image-duallog.jpg" width="100%"/>
        </figure>

        <p>
          In <xref ref="ps-image-planar02"/>, you will be asked to develop an equation for the streamlines of this flow. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-images-vortex-wall">
      <title>Line vortex near a wall</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the complex potential of a line vortex of strength <m>\Gamma</m> at <m>z = d</m> in the half-plane <m>x \gt 0</m>, bounded by a wall at <m>x = 0</m>, and verify the no-flux condition on the wall.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Near <m>z = d</m>, we expect <m>f(z) \sim -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi} \log(z - d)</m>, but this potential alone does not satisfy the no-flux condition at <m>x = 0</m>. We add an image vortex at <m>z = -d</m> of opposite sign:
          <me>
            f(z) = -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi} \log(z - d) + \frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi} \log(z + d).
          </me>
          For <m>\Gamma \gt 0</m>, this flow is composed of a line vortex circulating anticlockwise on the right, and a line vortex circulating clockwise on the left. The complex velocity is
          <me>
            u - \im v = -\frac{\im \Gamma d}{\pi(z^2 - d^2)},
          </me>
          and on <m>z = \im y</m>, <m>u - \im v = \im\Gamma d/[\pi(y^2 + d^2)]</m>, so <m>u = 0</m> and there is no flux through the wall.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

  <p>
    You will study a related problem with two vortices in <xref ref="ex-hw-image-planar03"/>.
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Uniqueness of solutions</title>
    <p>
      You may be wondering whether a potential found by the method of images, with its 'fictitious' image points, is the only solution. It is unique (up to a constant) once we prescribe the singularities in the flow region (sources and vortices, with their strengths), the no-flux condition on the walls, <em>and</em> the behaviour at infinity, e.g. <m>\bu \to \mathbf{0}</m>. This follows from the uniqueness of solutions to Laplace's equation with this type of boundary condition (a Neumann problem).
    </p>

    <p>
      The condition at infinity matters. In the examples above, adding a uniform flow parallel to the wall, <m>f = -\im Uz</m> (for which <m>\psi = -Ux</m> is constant on <m>x = 0</m>), still satisfies the no-flux condition, but changes the flow far from the source or vortex.
    </p>
  </remark>
  </subsection>

</section>

<section xml:id="sec-conformal-mapping">
  <title>Conformal mapping</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The essential idea of conformal mapping is as follows. Suppose that we are given a two-dimensional potential flow problem in a region, <m>R \subseteq \mathbb{C}</m>, with impermeable boundary <m>\partial R</m>. There may be singularities in <m>R</m> corresponding to sinks, sources, vortices, etc. We then seek a <em>conformal mapping</em> from the <m>z</m>-plane to the <m>\zeta</m>-plane via
      <me>
        \zeta = g(z),
      </me>
      so that the region <m>R</m> is mapped to a new region <m>\hat{R} \subseteq \mathbb{C}</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-conformal-general"/>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      The hope is that within the <m>\zeta</m>-plane, the fluid region is sufficiently simple that a complex potential, say <m>F(\zeta)</m>, can be found. This is aided by the fact that sources, sinks and vortices are preserved by the map (<xref ref="prop-conformal"/>). Typically, we wish for <m>\hat{R}</m> to be e.g. the upper half-plane or the unit disc, and <m>\partial\hat{R}</m> the real axis or circumference of the unit disc, respectively. Once found, the complex potential in the <m>z</m>-plane is obtained by composing <m>F</m> with the map, i.e. 
      <me>
        f(z) = F(g(z)) = \phi(x, y) + \im \psi(x, y).
      </me>
      This simple idea turns out to yield many insights into potential flows in two dimensions. 
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-general">
      <caption>A general conformal mapping from the <m>z</m>-plane to the <m>\zeta</m>-plane. The object is to map the region <m>R</m> to the region <m>\hat{R}</m>, which is geometrically simpler.</caption>
      <image source="conformal_generalmap.jpg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Conformal mapping</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-conformal-theory">
    <title>The conformal mapping method</title>

    <p>
      The method relies on the following properties of conformal maps.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-conformal-mapping">
      <title>Conformal map</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <em>conformal map</em> is a mapping <m>\zeta = g(z)</m> with <m>g</m> analytic and <m>g'(z) \neq 0</m> in a region <m>R</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The derivative <m>g'</m> may vanish or be infinite at corners of the boundary (e.g. <m>z^{\pi/\alpha}</m> at <m>z = 0</m> in <xref ref="example-conformal-wedge"/>), which is how the map straightens corners.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-conformal">
      <title>Properties of conformal maps</title>
      <statement>
        <p><ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Conformal maps preserve angles.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If the boundary <m>\partial{\hat{R}}</m> is a streamline in the <m>\zeta</m>-plane, then the corresponding boundary <m>\partial R</m> is a streamline in the <m>z</m>-plane (and vice versa). 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              A source of strength <m>Q</m> (or vortex of strength <m>\Gamma</m>) at <m>\zeta = g(c) \in \hat{R}</m> in the <m>\zeta</m>-plane corresponds to a source (or vortex) of the same strength at <m>z = c \in R</m> in the <m>z</m>-plane (and vice versa).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      </statement>

      <proof>
        <title>Proof (non-examinable)</title>
        <p>
          (1) Near a point <m>z_0</m>, <m>g(z) - g(z_0) \approx g'(z_0)(z - z_0)</m>, so small line elements are all rotated by <m>\arg g'(z_0)</m> and scaled by <m>|g'(z_0)|</m>; angles between them are unchanged. (2) If <m>\Im F</m> is constant on <m>\partial\hat{R}</m>, then <m>\Im f = \Im F(g(z))</m> is constant on <m>\partial R</m>. (3) Near <m>c</m>, <m>g(z) - g(c) = g'(c)(z - c)\big(1 + O(z - c)\big)</m>, so <m>\log(g(z) - g(c)) = \log(z - c)</m> plus a function analytic near <m>c</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Source in a wedge</title>

    <example xml:id="example-conformal-wedge">
      <title>Source in a wedge</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Fluid is contained in a wedge with walls at <m>\theta = 0</m> and <m>\theta = \alpha > 0</m>, with the fluid in <m>0 &lt; \theta &lt; \alpha</m>. Find the complex potential of a source of strength <m>Q</m> at the point <m>z = c</m> in the wedge.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Consider the map 
          <men xml:id="eqn-conformal-wedge-map">
           \zeta = g(z) = z^{\pi/\alpha}.
          </men>
          With <m>z = r\e^{\im\theta}</m>, we have <m>\zeta = r^{\pi/\alpha}\e^{\im\pi\theta/\alpha}</m>, so <m>0 &lt; \theta &lt; \alpha</m> is mapped to <m>0 &lt; \arg\zeta &lt; \pi</m>, the upper half-<m>\zeta</m>-plane. The ray <m>\theta = 0</m> is mapped to the positive real axis and the ray <m>\theta = \alpha</m> to the negative real axis, as shown in <xref ref="fig-conformal-wedge"/>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-wedge">
          <caption>The map from the wedge-shaped region in the <m>z</m>-plane (left) and the upper half-<m>\zeta</m>-plane (right). </caption>
          <image source="conformal_wedge.jpg" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>conformal map</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          In the <m>\zeta</m>-plane, we must solve for flow in the upper half-plane with a source of strength <m>Q</m> at <m>\zeta_c = c^{\pi/\alpha}</m>, and an impermeable boundary on the real <m>\zeta</m>-axis. By the method of images (<xref ref="sec-method-of-images"/>), we place sources at both <m>\zeta_c</m> and its complex conjugate, <m>\overline{\zeta_c}</m>:
          <men xml:id="eqn-conformal-zeta-wedge">
           F(\zeta) = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log(\zeta - \zeta_c) + \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log(\zeta - \overline{\zeta_c}).
          </men>
          Composing with the map, the complex potential in the <m>z</m>-plane is
          <men xml:id="eqn-conformal-z-wedge">
           f(z) = F(g(z)) = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log(z^{\pi/\alpha} - c^{\pi/\alpha}) + \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log(z^{\pi/\alpha} - \overline{c^{\pi/\alpha}}).
          </men>
          By construction (<xref ref="prop-conformal"/>), the walls of the wedge are streamlines.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Standard conformal maps</title>
   
    <p>
    The <em>exponential map</em> is used to map a channel to a half-plane. Consider a channel of width <m>h</m> in the region <m>0 &lt; \Im z &lt; h</m> in the <m>z</m>-plane. Then 
    <men xml:id="eqn-conformal-exp-map">
     \zeta = g(z) = \e^{\pi z/h},
    </men>
    maps this channel to the upper half-<m>\zeta</m>-plane. The correspondence of critical points and points at infinity in the pre-image and the image is shown in <xref ref="fig-conformal-exp"/>. It is good to see the map as essentially 'unfolding' the infinite channel, sending points AD to the origin, while sending B to negative infinity and C to positive infinity. The conformal map will preserve the orientation of the boundary, so as we traverse along ABCD, the fluid region is always on the right. 
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-exp">
      <caption>The exponential map maps the infinite strip of height <m>h</m> to the upper half-plane.</caption>
      <image source="conformal_exp.jpg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>conformal map</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      <em>Trigonometric maps</em> are used to map semi-infinite channels into a half-plane. For example, the map
      <men xml:id="eqn-conformal-sin-map">
        \zeta = g(z) = \sin\left(\frac{\pi z}{2a}\right)
      </men>
      sends the semi-infinite channel of width <m>2a</m> in <xref ref="fig-conformal-sin"/> to the upper half-plane. The two corners at <m>z = \pm a</m> are mapped to <m>\zeta = \pm 1</m>, respectively. 
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-sin">
      <caption>The sinusoidal transformation maps the semi-infinite strip of width <m>2a</m> to the upper half-plane.</caption>
      <image source="conformal_sin.jpg" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>conformal map</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      In <xref ref="ps-conformal-check-exp-sin"/>, you will verify this. 
    </p>

    <example xml:id="example-conformal-sinh-vortex">
      <title>Vortex flow in a semi-infinite channel</title>
     <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the channel shown in the left of <xref ref="fig-conformal-sinh"/>. Insert a vortex of strength <m>\Gamma</m> at the point <m>z = d \in \mathbb{R}^+</m>. Verify that an appropriate conformal map is given by 
        <men xml:id="eqn-conformal-sinh">
         \zeta = g(z) = \sinh\left(\frac{\pi z}{2a}\right),
        </men>
        and find where the map sends the relevant critical points of the pre-image. Using the conformal map, find the complex velocity of the flow. 
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-sinh">
        <caption>The sinh transformation maps the semi-infinite channel with height <m>2a</m> to the right half-plane. (There is a typo in the sketch: the vortex and its image in the <m>\zeta</m>-plane are at <m>\zeta = \pm\sinh(\pi d/2a)</m>, not <m>\zeta = \pm d</m>.)</caption>
        <image source="conformal_sinh.jpg" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>conformal map</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
     </statement> 

     <solution>
       <p>
         The map sends <m>z = 0</m> and <m>z = \pm\im a</m> to <m>\zeta = 0</m> and <m>\zeta = \pm\im</m>. On <m>x = 0</m>, <m>\zeta = \im\sin(\pi y/2a)</m> covers the segment between <m>\pm\im</m>, and on <m>y = \pm a</m>, <m>\zeta = \pm\im\cosh(\pi x/2a)</m> covers the rest of the imaginary axis. The channel is therefore mapped to the right half-plane, and the vortex to <m>\zeta_d = \sinh(\pi d/2a) \gt 0</m>.
       </p>

       <p>
         By the method of images (<xref ref="example-images-vortex-wall"/>), with an image vortex of strength <m>-\Gamma</m> at <m>-\zeta_d</m>,
         <me>
           F(\zeta) = -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi}\left[\log(\zeta - \zeta_d) - \log(\zeta + \zeta_d)\right].
         </me>
         The complex velocity is then
         <me>
           u - \im v = f'(z) = F'(\zeta)\, g'(z) = -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi}\,\frac{2\zeta_d}{\zeta^2 - \zeta_d^2}\cdot\frac{\pi}{2a}\cosh\left(\frac{\pi z}{2a}\right).
         </me>
         Using <m>\sinh^2 A - \sinh^2 B = \sinh(A - B)\sinh(A + B)</m>, this simplifies to
         <me>
           u - \im v = -\frac{\im\Gamma \sinh(\pi d/2a) \cosh(\pi z/2a)}{2a \sinh\big(\pi(z - d)/2a\big) \sinh\big(\pi(z + d)/2a\big)}.
         </me>
       </p>
     </solution>
    </example>
    
  </subsection>
</section>

  <section xml:id="sec-circle-maps">
    <title>Circle maps</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="sec-method-of-images"/>, we used the method of images to construct flows bounded by straight walls, and in <xref ref="sec-conformal-mapping"/> conformal maps extended this to wedges and channels. Many problems of interest instead involve flow past a body. In this section, we consider the simplest such body, a circular cylinder. Milne-Thomson's Circle Theorem provides the image system for a circle, much as the method of images does for a plane wall, and immediately gives uniform flow past a cylinder. We then add a line vortex inside the cylinder, which shifts the stagnation points and leads to the <em>Magnus effect</em>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-circle-theorem">
        <title>Milne-Thomson's Circle Theorem</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
          Let <m>f(z)</m> be a given complex potential such that any singularities in <m>f</m> occur in <m>|z| > a</m>. Then we can construct the following potential: 
          <men xml:id="eqn-milne-circle-map">
            w(z) = f(z) + \overline{f(a^2/\overline{z})},
          </men>
          and this potential has the following properties: (i) it has the same singularities as <m>f</m> in the region <m>|z| > a</m>; and (ii) the circle <m>|z| = a</m> is a streamline.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
          <p>
            (i) If <m>|z| &gt; a</m>, then <m>|a^2/\overline{z}| &lt; a</m>. Therefore the argument of the second term of <m>w</m> is within the circle of radius <m>a</m>, and by assumption <m>f</m> is not singular there. The second term is also analytic in <m>z</m>, since the two conjugations cancel.
          </p>

          <p>
            (ii) On the circle itself, <m>z = a\e^{\im\theta}</m>. 
            Then <m>a^2/\overline{z} = a\e^{\im\theta} = z</m>. Therefore, we have 
            <me>
            w(z) = f(z) + \overline{f(z)} = 2 \Re[f(z)].
            </me>
            Therefore the complex potential is entirely real. It follows that <m>\Im w = 0</m> on <m>|z| = a</m>, establishing that the circle is a streamline.
          </p> 
        </proof>
      </theorem>

     <p>
      The above theorem then allows us to easily construct certain flows past circular cylinders. Here is an example.
     </p>

     <example xml:id="example-uniform-past-circle">
      <title>Uniform flow past a circular cylinder</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the Circle Theorem to find the complex potential for uniform horizontal flow of speed <m>U</m> past a circular cylinder of radius <m>a</m>, centred at the origin.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We begin with the complex potential <m>f(z) = Uz</m>, which corresponds to horizontal uniform flow. It is non-singular for all finite <m>z</m>, so it satisfies the requirements of the Circle Theorem. Hence
          <men xml:id="eqn-potential-uniform-past-circle">
            w(z) = \phi + \im \psi = Uz + \overline{\frac{Ua^2}{\overline{z}}} = Uz + \frac{Ua^2}{z}.
          </men>
          The streamlines of the flow, found by taking the imaginary part of the above, are shown in <xref ref="fig-conformal-circle-flow"/>.
        </p>
        
        <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-circle-flow">
          <caption>Uniform flow past a circle</caption>
          <image source="conformal_circle_flow.png" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>Circle flow</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
     </example>

      <example xml:id="example-uniform-past-circle-with-vortex">
        <title>Uniform flow past a cylinder with circulation</title>
        
        <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the flow of <xref ref="example-uniform-past-circle"/>, with complex potential
          <men xml:id="eqn-flow-over-circle-problem">
            w(z) = Uz + \frac{Ua^2}{z}.
          </men>
        </p>
        </introduction>

          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Confirm, by use of the streamfunction <m>\psi</m>, that the circle boundary is a streamline of the flow. 
              </p>

              <p>
                It will be useful to remind yourself that 
                <men xml:id="eqn-complex-cosine">
                 \cos\theta = \frac{\e^{\im\theta} + \e^{-\im\theta}}{2}. 
                </men>
              </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
              <p>
                We set <m>z = a\e^{\im\theta}</m> into the complex potential. This gives 
                <me>
                  \phi + \im \psi = U a \left( \e^{\im \theta} + \e^{-\im\theta}\right) = 2Ua\cos\theta.
                </me>
               Therefore <m>\psi = \Im w = 0</m> on the surface of the cylinder.
              </p>
            </solution>
          </task>

          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Derive the complex velocity, <m>u - \im v</m>, and use it to show that on the surface of the cylinder, the velocity is given by 
                <me>
                  u - \im v = 2U \e^{\im (\pi/2 - \theta)} \sin\theta,
                </me>
                where <m>z = a\e^{\im\theta}</m>.                
              </p>

              <p>
                It is helpful for you to remember that 
                <men xml:id="eqn-complex-sine">
                  \sin\theta = \frac{\e^{\im\theta} - \e^{-\im\theta}}{2\im}.
                </men>                
              </p> 
            </statement>

            <solution>
              <p>
                Differentiating gives the complex velocity,
                <me>
                  w'(z) = u - \im v = U\left(1 - \frac{a^2}{z^2}\right).
                </me>
                Substituting <m>z = a\e^{\im\theta}</m> and simplifying gives 
                <me>
                  u - \im v = U \e^{-\im \theta}\left( \e^{\im\theta} - \e^{-\im\theta}\right) = 2U \e^{\im(\pi/2 - \theta)} \sin\theta.
                </me>
              </p>
            </solution>
          </task>

          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
               Conclude that on the surface of the cylinder, the maximum velocity is <m>|\bu| = 2U</m>. Where does this maximum velocity occur? Where do stagnation points in the flow (where the velocity is zero) occur?  
              </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
              <p>
                From the above, the magnitude is <m>|u - \im v| = 2U|\sin\theta|</m>, so the maximum speed on the cylinder is <m>2U</m>. This value is obtained at the top and bottom, <m>\theta = \pm \pi/2</m>. The stagnation points are where the speed is zero, at the fore and aft points, <m>\theta = \pi</m> and <m>\theta = 0</m>.
              </p>
            </solution>
          </task>

          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Show that on the surface of the cylinder, the pressure is given by
                <me>
                  p = p_\infty + \frac{1}{2} \rho U^2(1 - 4\sin^2\theta),
                </me>
                where <m>p_\infty</m> is the pressure at infinity.
              </p>
            </statement>

          <solution>
            <p>
              From Bernoulli's equation (<xref ref="thm-bernoulli-potential"/>), without gravity,
              <me>
                \left(p + \frac{\rho}{2} |\bu|^2\right)_{\text{cylinder}} = \left(p_\infty + \frac{\rho}{2} U^2\right)_{\text{infinity}},
              </me>
              where <m>p_\infty</m> is the pressure at infinity. Substituting the speed on the cylinder, <m>|\bu|^2 = 4U^2\sin^2\theta</m>, gives
              <me>
                p = p_\infty + \frac{\rho}{2}U^2(1 - 4\sin^2\theta)
              </me>
              as desired.
            </p>
          </solution>
          </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It is possible to add a line vortex to the interior of the circular cylinder, centred at <m>z = 0</m>, by adding to <xref ref="eqn-flow-over-circle-problem"/> an additional term: 
              <me>
                w(z) = U \left(z + \frac{a^2}{z}\right) - \frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi} \log z.
              </me>
              Verify again that the above modification does not change the streamline properties of <m>|z| = a</m>. 
            </p>

            <p>
              Does the above flow satisfy the condition that the flow is of uniform speed <m>U</m> far upstream? 
            </p>
          </statement>

          <solution>
            <p>
              The streamfunction of the vortex is constant on circles centred at the origin, so the cylinder remains a streamline. Indeed, on the surface, the added term is
              <me>
                -\frac{\im \Gamma}{2\pi} (\log a + \im \theta) = \frac{\Gamma\theta}{2\pi} - \frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi}\log a.
              </me>
             Therefore the imaginary part is still a constant, and hence the boundary of the cylinder is still a streamline of the flow.  
            </p>

            <p>
              To verify that the upstream flow is not altered, we take a derivative to obtain the complex velocity: 
                <me>
                  w'(z) = u - \im v = U\left(1 - \frac{a^2}{z^2}\right) - \frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi z}.
                </me>
                So in the limit <m>|z| \to \infty</m>, the velocity is horizontal with speed <m>U</m>.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task> 

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              By studying the velocity of the flow with the vortex added, verify that the complex velocity on the surface is given by 
              <me>
                u - \im v = \im \e^{-\im\theta}\left(2U \sin\theta  - \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi a}\right). 
              </me>
            </p>

            <p>
              Conclude that a point on the cylinder that satisfies the equation 
              <me>
                \sin\theta = \frac{\Gamma}{4\pi a U},
              </me>
              is a stagnation point of the flow.
            </p>
          </statement>

          <solution>
            <p>
              Using the previous task and adding the vortex term,
              <me>
                u - \im v = 2U \im \e^{-\im\theta}\sin\theta - \frac{\Gamma\im}{2\pi a} \e^{-\im\theta} = \im \e^{-\im\theta}
                \left(2U \sin\theta - \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi a}\right).
              </me>
            </p>

            <p>
              A stagnation point requires that <m>|u - \im v| = 0</m>, so setting the terms in brackets to zero implies 
              <me>
                \sin\theta = \frac{\Gamma}{4\pi a U},
              </me>
              as desired. This requires <m>|\Gamma| \le 4\pi aU</m>; for larger <m>|\Gamma|</m>, the stagnation point leaves the cylinder.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              (Not examinable) Use a computational tool to investigate the streamlines of the flow for <m>\Gamma = 0</m>, <m>2\pi aU</m>, <m>4\pi a U</m>, and <m>6\pi a U</m>. 
            </p>

            <p>
              The fact that the stagnation point shifts, moving on the top or bottom of the circular cylinder, depending on the value of <m>\Gamma</m>, will result in an asymmetry in the pressure, causing a net force on one side of the cylinder. This is related to the well-known <em>Magnus effect</em>, which is used by athletes to make a ball's path curve. 
            </p>
          </statement>

          <solution>
            <p>
              The point of this task is to note that when <m>\Gamma \neq 0</m>, the location of the stagnation point shifts. For example, if <m>\Gamma = 4\pi a U</m>, then the stagnation points are found at
              <me>
                \sin\theta = 1 \Longrightarrow \theta = \pi/2,
              </me>
              i.e. the top of the cylinder. For <m>\Gamma = 6\pi aU</m>, the stagnation point lies off the cylinder.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-circle-with-vortex">
              <caption>Flow past a circular cylinder with interior line vortex of different strengths.</caption>
              <image source="conformal_circle_with_vortex.png" width="100%">
                <shortdescription>Flow past a circular cylinder with vortex</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </task>
      </example>

    <remark xml:id="remark-joukowski">
      <title>Mapping the circle to an aerofoil</title>
      <p>
        In classical teaching of potential flow theory, one would also show how the circle can be conformally mapped to an aerofoil-like shape, including a flat plate, for example using the Joukowski map. The flows past a circular cylinder constructed in this section then give the flow past the aerofoil.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <aside>
      <title>Removed in 2026</title>
      <p>The material on the Joukowski map and flow past an aerofoil is not included in this year's delivery, and has been removed from the notes.</p>
    </aside>

    <!-- Removed in 2026: Joukowski map (not in this year's delivery)

<subsection xml:id="subsec-">
  <title>The Joukowski map</title>
  
    <p>
    We shall study the following mapping. 
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-joukowski">
      <statement>
        <p>
         The <em>Joukowski transformation</em> is the map 
         <men xml:id="eqn-joukowski">
          z = G(\zeta) = \zeta + \frac{a^2}{\zeta}
         </men>
         where <m>a &gt; 0</m> is a parameter. The mapping is conformal at all points except <m>\zeta = 0</m> (pole) and <m>\zeta = \pm a</m> where <m>G'(\zeta) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Consider a circle in the <m>\zeta</m>-plane. Let <m>\zeta = r\e^{\im\theta}</m>. Then in the <m>z</m>-plane, this is mapped to 
      <me>
        z = r\e^{\im\theta} + \frac{a^2}{r}\e^{-\im\theta}  = \left(r + \frac{a^2}{r}\right)\cos\theta + \im \left(r - \frac{a^2}{r}\right)\sin\theta. 
      </me>
      Provided that <m>r &gt; a</m>, this is the equation of an ellipse with principal radii <m>r + a^2/r</m> and <m>r - a^2/r</m>. The orientation of circles is preserved, with an anticlockwise rotation in <m>\zeta</m> corresponding to anticlockwise in <m>z</m>. The exterior of the circle <m>|\zeta| = r</m> is mapped to the exterior of the ellipse in <m>z</m>.</p>
      
      <p>In addition note that if <m>r \to a</m>, the ellipses tends to the line segment
      <me>
      S = \{ z = 2a\cos\theta \, | 0 \leq \theta \leq \pi\},
      </me>
      which then ranges from <m>z = 2a</m> for <m>\theta = 0</m> to <m>z = -2a</m> for <m>\theta = \pi</m>. As <m>\theta</m> further ranges in <m>[\pi, 2\pi)</m>, this traverses the horizontal plate again. One can interpret it as traversing the top or the bottom side of the plate (a view strengthened from considering the limiting images). This is shown in <xref ref="fig-conformal-joukowski01"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-joukowski01">
      <caption>The Joukowski transformation in <xref ref="eqn-joukowski"/> sends circles with radius <m>r \geq 1</m> to ellipses in the <m>z-</m>plane. In the limit <m>r \to a^+</m>, this approaches a horizontal plate of length <m>4a</m>.</caption>
      <image source="conformal_joukowski01.png" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>Joukowski map</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      If on the other hand, <m>r &lt; a</m>, then we can verify that the mapping still produces ellipses, but now with principal radii <m>r + a^2/r</m> and <m>a^2/r - r</m>. The orientation is now reversed, with anticlockwise orientation in <m>\zeta</m> mapped to clockwise orientation. Therefore, it is the case that the interior of the discs with <m>|\zeta| = r</m> are mapped to the exterior of ellipses in <m>z</m>. Again, in the limit that <m>r \to a^-</m> the images approach a flat plate of length <m>4a</m> on the axis. 
    </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-joukowski02">
      <caption>The Joukowski transformation in <xref ref="eqn-joukowski"/> sends circles with radius <m>r \leq 1</m> to ellipses in the <m>z-</m>plane. As <m>r \to \infty</m>, these tend to infinitely large circles. The values here are <m>r = 0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9, 1.</m></caption>
      <image source="conformal_joukowski02.png" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>Joukowski map</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>



    <p>
      We can invert <xref ref="eqn-joukowski"/>, and this gives
      <men xml:id="eqn-joukowski-inverted">
        \zeta = \frac{1}{2} \left(z \pm \sqrt{z^2 - 4a^2}\right) = 
        \frac{1}{2} \left(z \pm \sqrt{z-2a}\sqrt{z + 2a}\right).
      </men>
    As noted in <xref ref="sec-preliminary-complex-variables"/>, we must take care to define the proper branch cut structure for the two branch points at <m>z = \pm 2a</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      As noted in the exercises of <xref ref="ws-intro"/>, if we let 
      <me>
        z + 2a = r_1 \e^{\im \theta_1} \quad \text{and} \quad
        z - 2a = r_2 \e^{\im \theta_2}, 
      </me>
      and take <m>\theta_1, \theta_2 \in [0, 2\pi)</m>, this corresponds to a single branch cut between <m>z = -2a</m> and <m>z = 2a</m>. This branch cut choice is the most convenient since then it directly coincides with the central axis of the ellipse. 
    </p>

    <p>
      Once the proper branch structure has been chosen, we can define the two possible inverses of the mapping, corresponding to the positive and negative branches. We have 
      <mdn>
        <mrow>\zeta = g_+(z) \amp= \frac{1}{2}\left(z + \sqrt{z^2 - 4a^2}\right), </mrow>
        <mrow>\zeta = g_-(z) \amp= \frac{1}{2}\left(z - \sqrt{z^2 - 4a^2}\right). </mrow>
      </mdn>
    </p>

    <p>
      Let us return to the perspective of images from the <m>\zeta</m>-plane to the <m>z</m>-plane. We can now consider the images of circles in the <m>\zeta</m>-plane that have either been horizontally shifted and/or vertically shifted. The two critical points (points where the map is not conformal) mentioned above correspond to either <m>\zeta = \pm a</m> or <m>z = \pm 2a</m>. 
    </p>

    <p>
      Firstly in <xref ref="fig-conformal-joukowski03"/>, we see that horizontal shifts of the circle will corresponding to shifting and deforming the ellipse such that, as a critical point is approached, this forms a point of non-conformality in the image (a cusp).
    </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-joukowski03">
      <caption>We now consider shifting the circle with <m>|\zeta| = 2</m> to the left, so that the circumference passes through the critical point at <m>\zeta = a = 1</m>. This produces an image that resembles an aerofoil with a sharp trailing edge.</caption>
      <image source="conformal_joukowski03.png" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>Joukowski map</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Top-bottom asymmetry can also be introduced by considering vertical shifts of the preimages. 
    </p>

           <figure xml:id="fig-conformal-joukowski04">
      <caption>Vertical shifts correspond to changing the top-bottom symmetry of the aerofoil. In this case, notice that if the circle in the left plane passes through a critical point, this produces a self-intersecting shape.</caption>
      <image source="conformal_joukowski04.png" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>Joukowski map</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure> 
</subsection>   
    -->

  </section>




<exercises xml:id="ws-potentialflows">

  <introduction>
      <remark xml:id="remark-solutions-feedback-potentialflows">
        <title>Solutions and feedback</title>
        <p>Full solutions to these exercises can be found in <xref ref="solutions-potentialflows">the solutions to this chapter</xref> in <xref ref="solutions"/> at the back
          of the book.</p>

        <p>If you would like feedback on your work, the tutor will by default concentrate on the
          exercises highlighted with a yellow background in the online notes (the homework
          exercises), unless you specifically ask for feedback on a different exercise instead.</p>
      </remark>

 <p>
      There is an excellent <url href="https://potentialflow.com/">website at www.potentialflows.com</url> that allows you to plug in different flow elements (sources/sinks, vortices, etc.) into a potential flow and observe the streamlines and potential-flow lines. In your exercises, use this to help you visualise the flow.
    </p>

  </introduction>

  <exercisegroup>
    <title>Potential flows, part 1</title>
    
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      These exercises cover approximately around sections 4.1 (the velocity potential) to 4.3 (the complex potential). 
    </p>
  <!-- <warning>
  <p>We look to complete worksheets in the week prior to the content being delivered.
    Once this is done, this disclaimer message will be removed.</p>
</warning> -->
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-streamfunction-flux-compute">
    <title>Basic calculations</title>
    <introduction>

      <p>
        The following question relates to two-dimensional potential flow.  Remember that the fluid flux through a surface given by contour <m>C</m> is given by <xref ref="eqn-2d-flux"/>, or
        <me>
          \int_C \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{s}.
        </me>
        You will get some practice on calculating this quantity below. 
      </p>

      <p>
      For each of the following elementary flows, state or calculate: 

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
             the complex potential, <m>f(z)</m>; 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            the velocity vector, written in vector form <m>\bu = [u, v]</m>;
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            the flux through a surface consisting of a circle of unit radius;
          </p>

          <p>
            <em>Hint:</em> the unit normal for the circle is <m>\bn = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m>; when converting to polar coordinates remember that <m>\de{s} = r\de{\theta}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
           the flux through a surface consisting of a plate given by the line <m>y = -x + 1</m> with <m>0 \leq x \leq 1</m>.  
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Uniform flow of velocity <m>U</m> oriented at an angle of <m>\pi/4</m> to the horizontal.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p>
          <em>Circle.</em> Parametrise the unit circle by <m>[x, y] = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m> for <m>0 \leq \theta \lt 2\pi</m>. Explain why the outward unit normal is <m>\bn = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m> and why <m>\de{s} = \de\theta</m>, so that the flux becomes <m>\int_0^{2\pi} \bu \cdot \bn \, \de\theta</m>. Before integrating, can you predict the answer?
        </p>

        <p>
          <em>Plate.</em> Parametrise the plate by <m>\br(t) = [t, 1 - t]</m> for <m>0 \leq t \leq 1</m>. Show that the tangent is <m>\br'(t) = [1, -1]</m>, and hence that a unit normal, pointing away from the origin, is <m>\bn = [1, 1]/\sqrt{2}</m>. Show also that <m>\de{s} = |\br'(t)| \, \de{t} = \sqrt{2} \, \de{t}</m>, so that the flux becomes <m>\int_0^1 \bu \cdot \bn \, \sqrt{2} \, \de{t}</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>

      <solution>
        <p>
          (i) From <xref ref="example-potential-uniform"/> and <xref ref="subsec-complex-examples"/>, with <m>\alpha = \pi/4</m>,
          <me>
            f(z) = U\e^{-\im\alpha}z = U\e^{-\im \pi/4}z.
          </me>
        </p>
          
        <p>
          (ii) The complex velocity is <m>f'(z) = u - \im v = U\e^{-\im \pi/4}</m>, so
          <me>
            \bu = U \Bigl[ \cos(\pi/4), \, \sin(\pi/4) \Bigr] = \frac{U}{\sqrt{2}} [1, 1].
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          (iii) We expect the flux through the circle to be zero, since as much fluid enters one side as leaves the other. With <m>\bn = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m> and <m>\de{s} = \de\theta</m>,
          <me>
            \int_{r = 1} \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{s} = \frac{U}{\sqrt{2}} \int_0^{2\pi} (\cos\theta + \sin\theta) \, \de{\theta} = 0.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          (iv) Parametrising the plate by <m>\br(t) = [t, 1 - t]</m>, <m>0 \leq t \leq 1</m>, the tangent is <m>\br'(t) = [1, -1]</m>, so the unit normal pointing away from the origin is
          <me>
            \bn = \frac{[1, 1]}{\sqrt{2}},
          </me>
          and <m>\de{s} = |\br'(t)| \, \de{t} = \sqrt{2} \, \de{t}</m>. The flux is then
          <me>
            \int_0^1 \frac{U}{\sqrt{2}} [1, 1] \cdot \frac{[1, 1]}{\sqrt{2}} \, \sqrt{2} \, \de{t} = \sqrt{2}\,U.
          </me>
          This makes sense: the flow is everywhere normal to the plate, with speed <m>U</m>, and the plate has length <m>\sqrt{2}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A line source of strength <m>Q</m> located at the origin. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p>
          For the plate, use the same parametrisation and normal as in part (a), and write the velocity in Cartesian form, <m>\bu = \frac{Q}{2\pi}\frac{[x, y]}{x^2 + y^2}</m>. You may use, without proof, that
          <me>
            \int_0^1 \frac{\de{t}}{t^2 + (1 - t)^2} = \frac{\pi}{2}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </hint>

      <solution>
        <p>
          (i) From <xref ref="eqn-complex-linesource"/>,
          <me>
            f(z) = \frac{Q}{2\pi}\log z.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          (ii) The complex velocity is
          <md>
            <mrow>u - \im v = f'(z) \amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi z} = \frac{Q}{2\pi r}\e^{-\im\theta}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi r}(\cos\theta - \im\sin\theta),</mrow>
          </md>
          so
          <me>
            \bu = \frac{Q}{2\pi r}[\cos\theta, \sin\theta] = \frac{Q}{2\pi}\frac{[x, y]}{x^2 + y^2}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          (iii) On the unit circle, <m>r = 1</m>, <m>\bn = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m> and <m>\de{s} = \de\theta</m>, so
          <md>
            <mrow>\int_{r = 1} \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{s} \amp= \int_0^{2\pi} \frac{Q}{2\pi} [\cos\theta, \sin\theta] \cdot [\cos\theta, \sin\theta] \, \de\theta</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi} \de\theta = Q.</mrow>
          </md>
          This is the definition of the source strength: all of the fluid produced at the origin crosses the circle.
        </p>

        <p>
          (iv) As in part (a), parametrise the plate by <m>\br(t) = [t, 1 - t]</m>, <m>0 \leq t \leq 1</m>, with <m>\bn = [1, 1]/\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>\de{s} = \sqrt{2}\,\de{t}</m>. On the plate, <m>x = t</m> and <m>y = 1 - t</m>, so <m>x + y = 1</m> and <m>x^2 + y^2 = t^2 + (1 - t)^2</m>. Hence
          <md>
            <mrow>\textrm{flux} \amp= \int_0^1 \frac{Q}{2\pi}\frac{[x, y]}{x^2 + y^2} \cdot \frac{[1, 1]}{\sqrt{2}} \, \sqrt{2}\,\de{t}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi}\int_0^1 \frac{x + y}{x^2 + y^2}\,\de{t}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi}\int_0^1 \frac{\de{t}}{t^2 + (1 - t)^2}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi}\cdot\frac{\pi}{2} = \frac{Q}{4}.</mrow>
          </md>
          This makes sense: the source sends fluid out equally in all directions, and the plate spans a quarter of the directions seen from the origin (from <m>\theta = 0</m> to <m>\theta = \pi/2</m>), so it receives a quarter of the flux. In <xref ref="ex-hw-potential-fluxcalc"/>, you will see that this follows immediately from the streamfunction, <m>\psi = Q\theta/(2\pi)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Stagnation point flow with a stagnation point at the origin. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          (i) From <xref ref="subsec-complex-examples"/>,
          <me>
            f(z) = \frac{z^2}{2}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          (ii) The complex velocity is <m>f'(z) = z = x + \im y = u - \im v</m>, so
          <me>
            \bu = [x, -y].
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          (iii) On the unit circle, <m>[x, y] = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m>, <m>\bn = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m> and <m>\de{s} = \de\theta</m>, so
          <md>
            <mrow>\int_{r = 1} \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{s} \amp= \int_0^{2\pi} [\cos\theta, -\sin\theta] \cdot [\cos\theta, \sin\theta] \, \de\theta</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \int_0^{2\pi} (\cos^2\theta - \sin^2\theta) \, \de\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} \cos 2\theta \, \de\theta = 0.</mrow>
          </md>
          Fluid enters the circle near the <m>y</m>-axis and leaves near the <m>x</m>-axis, with no net flux.
        </p>

        <p>
          (iv) As in part (a), parametrise the plate by <m>\br(t) = [t, 1 - t]</m>, <m>0 \leq t \leq 1</m>, with <m>\bn = [1, 1]/\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>\de{s} = \sqrt{2}\,\de{t}</m>. On the plate, <m>\bu = [t, -(1 - t)]</m>, so
          <md>
            <mrow>\textrm{flux} \amp= \int_0^1 [t, -(1 - t)] \cdot \frac{[1, 1]}{\sqrt{2}} \, \sqrt{2}\,\de{t}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \int_0^1 (2t - 1) \, \de{t} = \Bigl[t^2 - t\Bigr]_0^1 = 0.</mrow>
          </md>
          This is consistent with <xref ref="thm-flux-streamlines"/>: the streamfunction <m>\psi = xy</m> is zero at both ends of the plate, <m>(0, 1)</m> and <m>(1, 0)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A line vortex flow of strength <m>\Gamma</m> placed at the origin. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p>
          For the plate, you should find an integral of the form <m>\int_0^1 \frac{2t - 1}{t^2 + (1 - t)^2}\,\de{t}</m>. Compare the numerator with the derivative of the denominator, or consider what happens under the substitution <m>t \mapsto 1 - t</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>

      <solution>
        <p>
          (i) From <xref ref="eq-line-vortex-complex-potential"/>,
          <me>
            f(z) = -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi}\log z.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          (ii) The complex velocity is
          <md>
            <mrow>u - \im v = f'(z) \amp= -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi z} = -\frac{\im\Gamma}{2\pi r}(\cos\theta - \im\sin\theta)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi r}(-\sin\theta - \im\cos\theta),</mrow>
          </md>
          so
          <me>
            \bu = \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi r}[-\sin\theta, \cos\theta] = \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi}\frac{[-y, x]}{x^2 + y^2}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          (iii) On the unit circle, <m>\bn = [\cos\theta, \sin\theta]</m> and <m>\de{s} = \de\theta</m>, so
          <md>
            <mrow>\int_{r = 1} \bu \cdot \bn \, \de{s} \amp= \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi} [-\sin\theta, \cos\theta] \cdot [\cos\theta, \sin\theta] \, \de\theta</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= 0.</mrow>
          </md>
          The integrand is zero: the flow is everywhere tangent to the circle, so no fluid crosses it.
        </p>

        <p>
          (iv) As in part (a), parametrise the plate by <m>\br(t) = [t, 1 - t]</m>, <m>0 \leq t \leq 1</m>, with <m>\bn = [1, 1]/\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>\de{s} = \sqrt{2}\,\de{t}</m>. On the plate, <m>-y + x = 2t - 1</m> and <m>x^2 + y^2 = t^2 + (1 - t)^2</m>, so
          <md>
            <mrow>\textrm{flux} \amp= \int_0^1 \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi}\frac{[-y, x]}{x^2 + y^2} \cdot \frac{[1, 1]}{\sqrt{2}} \, \sqrt{2}\,\de{t}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi}\int_0^1 \frac{2t - 1}{t^2 + (1 - t)^2}\,\de{t}.</mrow>
          </md>
          The denominator is <m>2t^2 - 2t + 1</m>, whose derivative is <m>2(2t - 1)</m>, so
          <me>
            \textrm{flux} = \frac{\Gamma}{4\pi}\Bigl[\log(2t^2 - 2t + 1)\Bigr]_0^1 = \frac{\Gamma}{4\pi}(\log 1 - \log 1) = 0.
          </me>
          Alternatively, under <m>t \mapsto 1 - t</m> the numerator changes sign while the denominator is unchanged, so the integral is zero. This is consistent with <xref ref="thm-flux-streamlines"/>: the streamfunction <m>\psi = -(\Gamma/2\pi)\log r</m> takes the same value at both ends of the plate, which are both at distance <m>r = 1</m> from the origin.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
        A flow consisting of a uniform flow of speed <m>U</m> moving horizontally to the right, combined with a line source of strength <m>Q</m> placed at the origin.   
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task> -->

    
  </exercise>

  
  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-potential-fluxcalc">
    <title>Evaluation of the flux</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Return to the previous question and, instead of directly calculating the flux via a line integral, use <xref ref="thm-flux-streamlines"/> to calculate the flux for the two geometries (unit circle and straight plate) for each of the flows given. 
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        This is a nice question to do because there is a simple recipe. For each of the complex potentials in the previous question, find the imaginary part to obtain the streamfunction. Then evaluate the difference in streamfunction values at the start and end values of the desired curve. 
      </p>

      <p>
        For uniform flow, <m>\psi(r, \theta) = Ur \sin(\theta - \alpha)|</m>. For the case of a circle, the flux is: 
        <me>
          |\psi(1, 2\pi) - \psi(1, 0)| = 0.
        </me>
        For the case of a plate, 
        <me>
          |\psi(1, 0) - \psi(1, \pi/2)| = U|\sin(-\pi/4) - \sin(\pi/4)| = \sqrt{2}U.
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        For source flow, <m>\psi = (Q/2\pi) \theta</m> once you convert the logarithm into its real and complex parts. Then for the circle:
        <me>
          |\psi(1, 2\pi) - \psi(1, 0)| = Q.
        </me>
        For the case of the plate,
        <me>
          |\psi(1, 0) - \psi(1, \pi/2)| = \frac{Q}{4}.
        </me>
        (Actually calculating the above might convince you the route of calculating flux by integrals is possible!).
      </p>

      <p>
        For the stagnation point, <m>\psi = (r^2/2)\sin 2\theta</m>. For the circle, 
        <me>
          |\psi(1, 2\pi) - \psi(1, 0)| = 0.
        </me>
        For the case of the plate, 
        <me>
          |\psi(1, 0) - \psi(1, \pi/2)| = 0.
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        For the case of the line vortex, <m>\psi = -\Gamma/(2\pi)\log r</m>, and for the circle, 
        <me>
          |\psi(1, 2\pi) - \psi(1, 0)| = 0,
        </me>
        while for the plate,
        <me>
          |\psi(1, 0) - \psi(1, \pi/2)| = 0.
        </me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise> 

   <exercise>
    <title>Doublet</title>
   
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A line source of strength <m>Q</m> is at <m>z = a</m> and a line sink of the same strength is at <m>z = -a</m> where <m>a > 0</m>. 
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write down the complex potential, <m>f(z)</m>. Find <m>f'(z)</m>. Locate any stagnation points and derive an equation for the streamlines of the flow. Finally, use a plotter, such as the one at <url href="https://potentialflow.com/">the potential flow simulator</url> to sketch the streamlines. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The complex potential is the sum of a line source and line sink:
          <me>
            f(z) = \frac{Q}{2\pi} [\log(z - a) - \log(z + a)].
          </me>
          Therefore the complex velocity is given by 
          <me>
            f'(z) = \frac{Q}{2\pi} \left[ \frac{1}{z-a} - \frac{1}{z+a}\right].
          </me>
          Stagnation points are where <m>f'(z) = 0</m>. There are no stagnation points. The streamfunction is given by 
          <me>
            \Im f = \frac{Q}{2\pi}(\theta_1 - \theta_2), 
          </me> 
          where <m>\theta_1</m> and <m>\theta_2</m> are angles measured relative to the positions about <m>\pm a</m>. Thus,
          <me>
            \theta_{1,2} = \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{y}{x \mp a} \right).
          </me>
         The streamlines are given where <m>\psi</m> is constant. The streamlines are shown below.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <caption>Streamlines for a sink and source</caption>
        <image source="potential_almostdoublet.png">
          <shortdescription>Almost doublet</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
    Let <m>a \to 0</m> and <m>Q \to \infty</m> while keeping the product <m>aQ</m> fixed. This gives the flow due to a <em>doublet</em><idx><h>doublet</h></idx>. Show that its complex potential is <m>\mu/z</m> where <m>\mu</m> is expressed in terms of <m>a</m> and <m>Q</m>. It will be useful for you to use the fact that 
   <me>
    \log(1 + \alpha) = \alpha + O(\alpha^2)
   </me>
   considered as an appropriate approximation when <m>\alpha</m> is small. 
    
    Show that the streamlines are circles through the origin with centres on the <m>y</m>-axis.           
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Expanding the logarithms for small <m>a</m> gives
          <me>
            \log z - \log z + \log(1 - a/z) - \log(1 + a/z) \sim -\frac{2a}{z}.
          </me>
         Thus in the limit <m>a \to 0</m>, we have
         <me>
          f(z) \sim -\frac{Qa}{\pi} \frac{1}{z}.
         </me>
         Therefore, we let both <m>a \to 0</m> and <m>Q \to \infty</m> in a fashion such that <m>m = aQ</m> is fixed. We then have the potential
         <me>
          f(z) = \frac{\mu}{z}
         </me>
         where <m>\mu = -Qa/\pi.</m>
         
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>

  </exercisegroup>

  <exercisegroup>
    <title>Potential flows, part 2</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        These exercises will cover the second part of Chapter 4, from sec. 4.4 (the method of images).
      </p>
    </introduction>
  
 <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-image-planar01">
      <title>Single source in a semi-infinite flow</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
       Verify that a single source of strength <m>Q</m> placed at the point <m>z = d > 0</m> is insufficient to describe flow bounded in the semi-infinite region, <m>x > 0</m>, with a planar boundary at <m>x = 0</m>. What is the horizontal and vertical velocities on the boundary? Find an equation for the streamlines and sketch the flow. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <image source="ex-potential-Q4.png">
          <shortdescription>Solution</shortdescription>
        </image>
        </solution>

        <p>
          There is a sign error in the above solution. In the last line of the $f'(z)$ computation, the sign of the <m>+iy</m> term has been flipped and should be <m>-iy</m>. This error propagates; the expression for <m>v</m> in the line that follows should have positive <m>Q</m>.
        </p>
    </exercise>
    
  <exercise xml:id="ps-image-planar02">
      <title>A source in a semi-infinite flow</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider the situation of two point sources of identical strength, <m>Q</m>, placed at <m>z = \pm d</m>, with <m>d \gt 0</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Develop equations for the complex potential, <m>f(z) = \phi + \im \psi</m>, and complex velocity, <m>u - \im v</m>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <em>Note:</em> the algebra quickly becomes awkward, whatever form you use. In this part, you are not expected to simplify beyond writing the streamfunction in a form such as <m>\psi = \frac{Q}{2\pi}\operatorname{Arg}\big[(x + \im y)^2 - d^2\big]</m>. You may then confirm the shape of the streamlines, <m>\psi = \textrm{constant}</m>, with a plot.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <image source="ex-potential-Q5.png">
            <shortdescription>Solution</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>\beta</m> denote the constant value of <m>\operatorname{Arg}(z^2 - d^2)</m> along a given streamline. Show that, on this streamline,
            <me>
              \frac{2xy}{x^2 - y^2 - d^2} = \tan\beta.
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>

        <hint>
          <p>
            Start from part (a): explain why <m>\psi = \textrm{constant}</m> means that <m>\operatorname{Arg}(z^2 - d^2)</m> is constant along a streamline.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, substitute <m>z = x + \im y</m> and expand <m>z^2 - d^2</m> to find its real and imaginary parts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, recall that any complex number can be written as <m>w = a + \im b = |w|\e^{\im\beta}</m>, where <m>\beta = \operatorname{Arg} w</m>. Comparing real and imaginary parts gives <m>a = |w|\cos\beta</m> and <m>b = |w|\sin\beta</m>, so that <m>b/a = \tan\beta</m>.
          </p>
        </hint>

        <solution>
          <p>
            Since <m>\psi = \frac{Q}{2\pi}\operatorname{Arg}(z^2 - d^2)</m>, a streamline <m>\psi = \textrm{constant}</m> is a curve on which <m>\operatorname{Arg}(z^2 - d^2) = \beta</m> is constant. With <m>z = x + \im y</m>,
            <me>
              z^2 - d^2 = (x^2 - y^2 - d^2) + 2\im xy.
            </me>
            Writing <m>z^2 - d^2 = |z^2 - d^2|\e^{\im\beta}</m>, the real and imaginary parts are <m>|z^2 - d^2|\cos\beta</m> and <m>|z^2 - d^2|\sin\beta</m>. Taking their ratio,
            <me>
              \frac{2xy}{x^2 - y^2 - d^2} = \tan\beta.
            </me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Hence show that the streamlines are hyperbolae, and find their equation.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <hint>
          <p>
            Multiply out the result of part (b), writing <m>k = \cot\beta</m>, and rearrange it into the form <m>Ax^2 + Bxy + Cy^2 = D</m>. Recall that such a conic is a hyperbola when <m>B^2 - 4AC \gt 0</m>.
          </p>
        </hint>

        <solution>
          <p>
            Multiplying out and writing <m>k = \cot\beta</m>, the streamlines are
            <me>
              x^2 - 2kxy - y^2 = d^2.
            </me>
            This is a conic with <m>A = 1</m>, <m>B = -2k</m> and <m>C = -1</m>, so <m>B^2 - 4AC = 4k^2 + 4 \gt 0</m>, and each streamline is a hyperbola. Since <m>A + C = 0</m>, these are rectangular hyperbolae, and all of them pass through the two sources at <m>(\pm d, 0)</m>. The limiting case <m>\tan\beta = 0</m> gives <m>xy = 0</m> away from the sources, i.e. the coordinate axes; in particular, the wall <m>x = 0</m> is a streamline, as required.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  
  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-image-planar03">
      <title>Two vortices and a dividing boundary</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the situation of two point vorticies of identical strength, but opposite direction, placed at <m>z = \pm d</m>, with <m>d \gt 0</m>. Develop equations for the complex potential, <m>f(z) = \phi + \im \psi</m>, and complex velocity, <m>u - \im v</m>. 
        </p>

        <p>
          Demonstrate that the streamlines are given by 
          <me>
            \psi = \frac{\Gamma}{2\pi}\log \left( \frac{r_2}{r_1}\right).
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <image source="ex-potential-Q6.png">
          <shortdescription>Solution</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </exercise>


    <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-linesource-stagnation">
      <title>A line source in a flow; stagnation points</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
              Incompressible inviscid fluid occupies the region <m>y > 0</m>, and there is a rigid plane
wall at <m>y = 0</m>. There is a uniform flow, speed <m>U</m>, in the positive <m>x</m>-direction, and a
line source of strength <m>Q</m> at <m>(0, a)</m>, where <m>a &gt; 0</m>. Find the complex potential <m>f(z)</m>
and calculate <m>f'(z)</m>. Let <m>\beta = Q/(2\pi aU)</m>. Show that if <m>\beta &gt; 1</m> there are two stagnation
points, both on the wall, while if <m>\beta &lt; 1</m> there is only one, in the fluid, a distance <m>a</m>
from the origin. Try to sketch the streamlines in either case  
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The potential is given by 
          <me>
            f(z) = Uz + \frac{Q}{2\pi} [\log(z - a\im) + \log(z + a\im)] = Uz + \frac{Q}{2\pi} \log(z^2 + a^2).
          </me>
          The complex velocity is then 
          <me>
            f'(z) = U + \frac{Qz}{\pi(z^2 + a^2)}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          A stagnation point requires <m>f'(z) = 0</m> and so we solve the resultant quadratic to obtain 
          <me>
            z = \frac{-Q \pm \sqrt{Q^2 - (2a\pi U)^2}}{2\pi U}.
          </me>
          Setting <m>\beta = Q/(2\pi a U)</m>, there are two real roots if <m>\beta &gt; 1</m>. Hence this is the case of two stagnation points along the axis. If <m>\beta &lt; 1</m>, then there two complex conjugate points, and hence only one stagnation point in the flow field. 
        </p>

        <figure>
          <caption><m>\beta = 0.94</m></caption>
          <image source="potential_stag_2_inflow.png" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>image</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        
        <figure>
          <caption><m>\beta = 1.3</m></caption>
          <image source="potential_stag_2_onbound.png" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>image</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id='ps-conformal-check-exp-sin'>
      <title>The exponential and sinusoidal conformal maps</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Check where each of the labeled points in the associated diagrams for the exponential map <xref ref="eqn-conformal-exp-map"/> and <xref ref="eqn-conformal-sin-map"/> are sent, from the <m>z</m>-plane to the <m>\zeta</m>-plane. In each of these cases, verify that an interior (within the fluid domain) is sent to the upper half-plane. 
        </p> 
        
        <p>
          Construct the complex potential that corresponds to a single line source placed within the fluid in each of these cases (an infinite strip of height <m>h</m> and a semi-infinite strip). 
        </p>
      </statement>

     <solution>
      <p>
      For the exponential map, <m>\zeta = \e^{\pi z/h}</m>, we want to check the outputs of points A, B, C, D listed on the figure. These are as follows.
      </p>
      <table>
        <title>Exponential map</title>
        <tabular>
          <row>
            <cell><m>z</m>-plane</cell>
            <cell><m>\zeta</m>-plane</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-\infty + \im h</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>0^-</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\infty + \im h</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>-\infty</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\infty</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>+\infty</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-\infty</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>0^+</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        When thinking of the map, the most important step is to just verify that the line, <m>z = t + \im h</m>, is mapped to <m>\zeta = -\e^{\pi t/h}</m>, so the effect of shifting vertically by <m>\im h</m> is to negate the values as compared to evaluating on <m>z = t</m>. To verify the region the fluid is in, simply "walk" from ABCD in the <m>z</m>-plane, and remark on the orientation of the fluid relative to your journey (the fluid is on the right). The direction is preserved also in the <m>\zeta</m>-plane. 
      </p>

      <p>
        The potential that corresponds to a single source is found by using method of images in the <m>\zeta</m> plane and mapping backwards. It is
        <md>
          <mrow>F(\zeta) \amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi} \left[\log(\zeta - d) + \log(\zeta - \overline{d})\right], </mrow>
          <mrow>f(z) \amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi} \left[\log(\e^{\pi z/h} - d) + \log(\e^{\pi z/h} - \overline{d})\right], </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        For the trigonometric map, <m>\zeta = \sin(\pi z/2a)</m>, the work is similar. 
      </p>

      <table>
        <title>Trigonometric map</title>
        <tabular>
          <row>
            <cell><m>z</m>-plane</cell>
            <cell><m>\zeta</m>-plane</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-a + \im \infty</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>-\infty</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>-a</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>0</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>a</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>a + \im \infty</m> </cell>
            <cell><m>\infty</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The potential that corresponds to a single source is found by using method of images in the <m>\zeta</m> plane and mapping backwards. It is
        <md>
          <mrow>F(\zeta) \amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi} \left[\log(\zeta - d) + \log(\zeta - \overline{d})\right], </mrow>
          <mrow>f(z) \amp= \frac{Q}{2\pi} \left[\log(\sin(\pi z/2a) - d) + \log(\sin(\pi z/2a) - \overline{d})\right], </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

     </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-hw-conformal-elementary">
        <title>Elementary conformal maps</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Define the term <em>conformal map</em>. 
        </p>

        <p>
          Write down the conformal maps from the wedge <m>0 &lt; \textrm{arg}(z) &lt; \alpha</m> into the upper half-plane. Find all the points at which the map is not conformal. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          A conformal map is a mapping <m>\zeta = g(z)</m> where <m>g</m> is analytic and its derivative is nonzero in some region of the complex plane. 
        </p>

        <p>
          The map from the wedge to the upper half-plane is given in the notes near <xref ref="eqn-conformal-wedge-map"/>. It is <m>\zeta = z^{\pi/\alpha}</m>. The points where the mapping is not conformal are the critical points listed in the figure <xref ref="fig-conformal-wedge"/>. Point A at infinity is mapped directly to +infinity. The origin is mapped to the origin. And the point, B, found via <m>z = r\e^{\im\alpha}</m>, is sent to <m>\zeta = -r^{\pi/\alpha}</m> and then afterwards <m>r \to \infty</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
      
    </exercise>

      <exercise>
        <title>Flow past a flat plate</title>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that the circle <m>|\zeta| = a</m> is mapped to a line segment 
              <me>
                S = \{ z: \Im z = 0, -2a \leq \Re z \leq 2a\}
              </me>
              along the real <m>z</m>-axis by the Joukowski transformation 
              <me>
                z = \zeta + \frac{a^2}{\zeta}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </statement>

          <solution>
            <image source="ex-potential-Q10a.png">
              <shortdescription>Solution</shortdescription>
            </image>
          </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Deduce that the exterior of the line segment is mapped to the exterior of the circle <m>|\zeta| = a</m> by the transformation 
              <me>
                \zeta = \frac{1}{2}\left( z + \sqrt{z^2 - 4a^2}\right).
              </me>
              Carefully define the function <m>\sqrt{z^2 - 4a^2}</m> and determine where the mapping above is conformal.
            </p>
          </statement>

          <solution>
            <image source="ex-potential-Q10b.png">
              <shortdescription>Solution</shortdescription>
            </image>
          </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Construct a flow, by providing the complex potential <m>\phi + \im \psi</m>, that consists of: uniform flow oriented at an angle <m>\alpha</m> to the horizontal at infinity; a line source of strength <m>Q</m> placed in the flow; and a horizontal flat plate of length <m>4a</m> on the axis, <m>-2a &lt; z &lt; 2a</m>. Sketch what you believe will be the streamlines of such a flow.
            </p>
          </statement>

          <solution>
            <p>
              First, construct the complex potential <m>F(\zeta)</m> of uniform flow plus a source. Use the Circle Theorem (<xref ref="thm-circle-theorem"/>) to obtain the corresponding flow past a circular cylinder of radius <m>a</m>. Finally, use the map from part (b) to obtain the flow in the <m>z</m>-plane.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ex-flatplate-solution">
              <caption>Construction of the flow past a flat plate. There are two typos in the sketch: in the Circle Theorem step, the second term should read <m>\overline{F(a^2/\overline{\zeta})}</m>, and the final map should read <m>\zeta = g(z) = \frac{1}{2}\left(z + \sqrt{z - 2a}\sqrt{z + 2a}\right)</m>, as in part (b).</caption>
              <image source="ex-potential-Q10c.png">
                <shortdescription>Solution</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </task>
        
      </exercise>

